      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{3}{7+\\sqrt{a}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $7-\\sqrt{a}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $7-\\sqrt{a},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7+\\sqrt{a}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{7+\\sqrt{a}}\\cdot \\frac{7-\\sqrt{a}}{7-\\sqrt{a}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(7-\\sqrt{a})}{(7+\\sqrt{a})(7-\\sqrt{a})} & = \\\\ \\frac{21-3\\sqrt{a}}{{7}^{2}-(\\sqrt{a})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{21-3\\sqrt{a}}{49-a}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
